      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE/OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To determine whether improved monitoring through close follow-up with a nurse practitioner (NP) could enhance treatment compliance and decrease frequency of hospitalizations."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective chart review."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "An academic National Cancer Institute-designated comprehensive cancer center."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SAMPLE",
          "content": "151 patients aged 45-65 years diagnosed with stage III or IV oropharyngeal cancer."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Patients were nonrandomized to one of two groups: a prechemotherapy clinic group and a weekly NP-led clinic group. After examination of descriptive statistics, multiple linear and logistic regressions were used to compare groups across patient outcomes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN RESEARCH VARIABLES",
          "content": "Hospitalization, chemotherapy dose deviations, and chemotherapy treatment completion."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "FINDINGS",
          "content": "The average number of visits during traditional treatment was three and, after initiation of the NP-led clinic, the number was six. The hospitalization rate was 28% in the traditional clinic group compared to 12% in the NP-led group. The rate of chemotherapy dose deviations was 48% in the traditional clinic group compared to 6% in the NP-led clinic group. Forty-six percent of patients in the traditional clinic group received the full seven scheduled doses of chemotherapy compared to 90% of patients seen in the NP-led clinic group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "A weekly NP-led symptom management clinic reduces rates of hospitalization and chemotherapy dose deviations and increases chemotherapy completion in patients receiving intensive chemoradiotherapy for oropharyngeal cancer."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "IMPLICATIONS FOR NURSING",
          "content": "Patients receiving chemoradiotherapy benefit from close monitoring for toxicities by NPs to successfully complete their treatment and avoid hospitalization."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "KNOWLEDGE TRANSLATION",
          "content": "Early interventions to manage toxicities in patients with head and neck cancer can improve outcomes. NPs are in a key position to manage these toxicities and, when symptoms are controlled, costs are reduced."
        }
      ]},
